# Green's Function of the Laplacian on $\mathbb{R}^n$

We seek a distribution $g \in \mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^n)$ satisfying

$$-\Delta g = \delta_0, \qquad \text{i.e.}\quad \langle -\Delta g, \varphi\rangle = \varphi(0) \quad \forall\,\varphi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n).$$

(The sign convention $-\Delta g=\delta$ is the standard one in PDE; with $+\Delta g=\delta$ one flips an overall sign.) We treat the cases $n\ge 3$, $n=2$, and $n=1$ together via a unified argument, then comment on $n\ge 3$ being the cleanest.

## 1. Radial reduction

The Laplacian and the delta are both rotation-invariant, so any fundamental solution is radial up to an additive harmonic function. We look for a radial $g(x)=\gamma(r)$, $r=|x|$, away from the origin. For $r>0$ the equation becomes

$$-\Delta\gamma(r)=0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \gamma''(r)+\frac{n-1}{r}\gamma'(r)=0,\qquad r>0.$$

Setting $u=\gamma'$ gives $u'+\frac{n-1}{r}u=0$, hence $u(r)=C\,r^{-(n-1)}$ and

$$\gamma(r)=
\begin{cases}
C_1\,r^{2-n}+C_2, & n\ge 3,\\[4pt]
C_1\,\log r+C_2, & n=2,\\[4pt]
C_1\,r+C_2, & n=1.
\end{cases}$$

We choose the integration constants so that $g$ is **tempered** (so it defines a distribution via integration against Schwartz functions) and **vanishes at infinity** when possible:

- $n\ge 3$: take $C_2=0$ (so $g\to 0$ as $r\to\infty$), leaving $\gamma(r)=C\,r^{2-n}$.
- $n=2$: $\log r$ is unbounded both at $0$ and $\infty$; it is still locally integrable and tempered, so we keep $\gamma(r)=C\,\log r$ (the additive constant is a free harmonic function, fixed by convention; the standard choice is $\gamma(r)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\log r$).
- $n=1$: $\gamma(r)=C\,|x|$ (using $r=|x|$, and choosing the odd, vanishing-at-infinity-impossible option; $|x|$ is the tempered fundamental solution).

## 2. Determining the constant $C$

We need $\langle -\Delta g,\varphi\rangle=\varphi(0)$. Equivalently, integrating $-\Delta g$ over a ball $B_\varepsilon$ and using the divergence theorem isolates the singularity.

### Case $n\ge 3$

Let $\gamma(r)=C\,r^{2-n}$ and compute the flux through $\partial B_R$:

$$\int_{B_R}-\Delta g\,dx = -\int_{\partial B_R}\partial_\nu g\,dS = -\int_{\partial B_R}\gamma'(R)\,dS.$$

Since $\gamma'(r)=C(2-n)\,r^{1-n}$ and $|\partial B_R|=\omega_{n-1}R^{n-1}$ (with $\omega_{n-1}=|\mathbb{S}^{n-1}|=2\pi^{n/2}/\Gamma(n/2)$),

$$-\int_{\partial B_R}\gamma'(R)\,dS = -C(2-n)R^{1-n}\cdot\omega_{n-1}R^{n-1}=C(n-2)\,\omega_{n-1}.$$

This flux is independent of $R$ (as expected, since $\Delta g=0$ away from $0$), and equals the total mass of $-\Delta g$, which must be $1$ (the mass of $\delta_0$). Hence

$$C(n-2)\omega_{n-1}=1 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad C=\frac{1}{(n-2)\,\omega_{n-1}}=\frac{\Gamma(n/2)}{2\,\pi^{n/2}(n-2)}=\frac{1}{n(2-n)\,|\mathbb{B}^n|}\cdot(\text{consistent form}).$$

Using the standard normalization $\omega_{n-1}=2\pi^{n/2}/\Gamma(n/2)$,

$$\boxed{\;g(x)=\frac{1}{(n-2)\,\omega_{n-1}}\,|x|^{2-n}=\frac{\Gamma(n/2)}{2(n-2)\pi^{n/2}}\,|x|^{2-n},\qquad n\ge 3.\;}$$

### Case $n=2$

Now $\gamma(r)=C\log r$, so $\gamma'(r)=C/r$ and

$$-\int_{\partial B_R}\gamma'(R)\,dS=-\frac{C}{R}\cdot 2\pi R=-2\pi C.$$

Setting $-2\pi C=1$ gives $C=-\dfrac{1}{2\pi}$, so

$$\boxed{\;g(x)=-\frac{1}{2\pi}\log|x|,\qquad n=2.\;}$$

(The sign: $-\Delta(-\frac1{2\pi}\log|x|)=\delta_0$. Equivalently $\Delta\frac{1}{2\pi}\log|x|=\delta_0$.)

### Case $n=1$

$\gamma(r)=C|x|$, $\gamma'(x)=C\,\mathrm{sgn}(x)$, $\gamma''=2C\,\delta_0$. The equation $-\gamma''=\delta_0$ gives $-2C=1$, i.e. $C=-\tfrac12$:

$$\boxed{\;g(x)=-\tfrac12|x|,\qquad n=1.\;}$$

## 3. Verification (distributional)

We verify the $n\ge 3$ case rigorously; the others are identical in spirit. For $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n)$, set $G(x)=c_n|x|^{2-n}$ with $c_n=1/((n-2)\omega_{n-1})$. For $\varepsilon>0$,

$$\langle -\Delta G,\varphi\rangle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^n}G\,(-\Delta\varphi)\,dx=\lim_{\varepsilon\to0}\int_{\mathbb{R}^n\setminus B_\varepsilon}G\,(-\Delta\varphi)\,dx.$$

On $\mathbb{R}^n\setminus B_\varepsilon$, $\Delta G=0$, so integrating by parts twice (boundary terms on $\partial B_\varepsilon$ only, since $\varphi$ is compactly supported):

$$\int_{\mathbb{R}^n\setminus B_\varepsilon}G\,(-\Delta\varphi)\,dx=\int_{\mathbb{R}^n\setminus B_\varepsilon}(-\Delta G)\varphi\,dx+\underbrace{\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\bigl(G\,\partial_\nu\varphi-\varphi\,\partial_\nu G\bigr)\,dS}_{\text{boundary}}.$$

The first term vanishes because $\Delta G=0$ there. For the boundary term on $\partial B_\varepsilon$ (outward normal of the *exterior* domain points **into** the hole, $\nu=-\hat r$):

- $\displaystyle\Bigl|\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}G\,\partial_\nu\varphi\,dS\Bigr|\le \|\nabla\varphi\|_\infty\,c_n\,\varepsilon^{2-n}\cdot\omega_{n-1}\varepsilon^{n-1}=c_n\omega_{n-1}\|\nabla\varphi\|_\infty\,\varepsilon\to0.$
- $\displaystyle -\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,\partial_\nu G\,dS=-\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,(-\partial_r G)\,dS=\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,\partial_r G\,dS.$

Now $\partial_r G=c_n(2-n)r^{1-n}$, so with $\nu=-\hat r$ we have $\partial_\nu G=-\partial_r G=-c_n(2-n)\varepsilon^{1-n}=c_n(n-2)\varepsilon^{1-n}$. Thus

$$-\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,\partial_\nu G\,dS=-c_n(n-2)\varepsilon^{1-n}\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,dS=-c_n(n-2)\,\omega_{n-1}\cdot\Bigl(\frac{1}{\omega_{n-1}\varepsilon^{n-1}}\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,dS\Bigr).$$

The quantity in parentheses is the spherical average of $\varphi$ over $\partial B_\varepsilon$, which tends to $\varphi(0)$ as $\varepsilon\to0$. Using $c_n(n-2)\omega_{n-1}=1$,

$$\lim_{\varepsilon\to0}-\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,\partial_\nu G\,dS=-\varphi(0).$$

Wait — we must track signs carefully. Re-collecting: the boundary term is $\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}(G\partial_\nu\varphi-\varphi\partial_\nu G)\,dS$. The first piece $\to0$. The second piece:

$$-\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,\partial_\nu G\,dS,\qquad \partial_\nu G=\nu\cdot\nabla G=(-\hat r)\cdot\bigl(c_n(2-n)\varepsilon^{1-n}\hat r\bigr)=-c_n(2-n)\varepsilon^{1-n}=c_n(n-2)\varepsilon^{1-n}.$$

So

$$-\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,\partial_\nu G\,dS=-c_n(n-2)\varepsilon^{1-n}\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,dS\to -c_n(n-2)\omega_{n-1}\,\varphi(0)=-\varphi(0).$$

But we want $\langle -\Delta G,\varphi\rangle=\varphi(0)$, and we computed $\langle -\Delta G,\varphi\rangle=\lim\int_{\mathbb{R}^n\setminus B_\varepsilon}G(-\Delta\varphi)\,dx$, which equals the boundary term $\to -\varphi(0)$. The sign discrepancy is resolved by re-examining the integration-by-parts identity: starting from $\int_{\Omega_\varepsilon}G(-\Delta\varphi)$ and integrating by parts once,

$$\int_{\Omega_\varepsilon}G(-\Delta\varphi)=\int_{\Omega_\varepsilon}\nabla G\cdot\nabla\varphi-\int_{\partial\Omega_\varepsilon}G\,\partial_\nu\varphi\,dS,$$

and a second time,

$$=\int_{\partial\Omega_\varepsilon}\varphi\,\partial_\nu G\,dS-\int_{\Omega_\varepsilon}(\Delta G)\varphi\,dS-\int_{\partial\Omega_\varepsilon}G\,\partial_\nu\varphi\,dS.$$

Since $\Delta G=0$ in $\Omega_\varepsilon$, only the boundary survives:

$$\int_{\Omega_\varepsilon}G(-\Delta\varphi)=\int_{\partial\Omega_\varepsilon}\varphi\,\partial_\nu G\,dS-\int_{\partial\Omega_\varepsilon}G\,\partial_\nu\varphi\,dS.$$

With $\partial_\nu G=c_n(n-2)\varepsilon^{1-n}$ (computed above), the leading term is

$$\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,\partial_\nu G\,dS=c_n(n-2)\varepsilon^{1-n}\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon}\varphi\,dS\to c_n(n-2)\omega_{n-1}\varphi(0)=\varphi(0),$$

and the $G\partial_\nu\varphi$ term $\to0$. Therefore

$$\langle -\Delta G,\varphi\rangle=\lim_{\varepsilon\to0}\int_{\Omega_\varepsilon}G(-\Delta\varphi)\,dx=\varphi(0)=\langle\delta_0,\varphi\rangle.$$

This confirms $-\Delta G=\delta_0$ in $\mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^n)$. The same flux computation with the constants above verifies $n=2$ and $n=1$.

## 4. Uniqueness (up to harmonic functions)

If $g_1,g_2$ both satisfy $-\Delta g_i=\delta_0$, then $h=g_1-g_2$ is entire harmonic: $\Delta h=0$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$. Requiring $g$ to be **tempered** forces $h$ to be a tempered harmonic function, hence a harmonic polynomial; further requiring $g\to0$ at infinity (possible for $n\ge3$) forces $h\equiv0$. For $n=2,1$ the additive harmonic freedom is fixed by convention (e.g. requiring $g$ to be radial and have a specified normalization). Thus the formulas above are the **canonical** Green's functions.

## 5. Summary

$$g(x)=
\begin{cases}
-\dfrac12\,|x|, & n=1,\\[8pt]
-\dfrac{1}{2\pi}\,\log|x|, & n=2,\\[10pt]
\dfrac{1}{(n-2)\,\omega_{n-1}}\,|x|^{2-n}=\dfrac{\Gamma(n/2)}{2(n-2)\pi^{n/2}}\,|x|^{2-n}, & n\ge 3,
\end{cases}$$

where $\omega_{n-1}=|\mathbb{S}^{n-1}|=2\pi^{n/2}/\Gamma(n/2)$. Each satisfies $-\Delta g=\delta_0$ in $\mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^n)$, is radial, tempered, and (for $n\ge3$) vanishes at infinity; uniqueness up to additive entire harmonic functions, fixed to a canonical representative by the tempered/vanishing-at-infinity conditions.

